00:48:18,610 --> 00:48:20,861
Speaker 1: And it looked like it was erased, but it wasn't.

710
00:48:21,022 --> 00:48:22,510
Speaker 1: And then I used the app on the test.

711
00:48:24,230 --> 00:48:36,750
Speaker 0: But factorization, I want to say for complex equations, edges into like hard mathematical problem, because sometimes it can only be done by trial and error, depending on the complexity.

712
00:48:37,770 --> 00:48:53,950
Speaker 0: But the fundamental idea that we want to try to apply to games and maybe we'll do this to a couple of games as an example in the panel is just start arbitrarily breaking a game down and follow some of the threads to break all the way down to a handful of very simple mechanics.

713
00:48:54,831 --> 00:49:15,150
Speaker 0: So an example would be Cobalt Core, a game we reviewed recently, which we could easily say, if we simplify a little bit, is fundamentally a combination of FTL the game, Faster Than Light, crossed with Slay the Spire the game, and then plus a bunch of other small games that we're not going to worry about in our factorization.

714
00:49:15,230 --> 00:49:16,532
Speaker 0: But those are like the two main games.

715
00:49:17,113 --> 00:49:28,550
Speaker 0: But Slay the Spire can be broken down to effectively a deck builder crossed with a single player puzzle game, crossed with a roguelike, like a dungeon cross.

716
00:49:28,690 --> 00:49:34,003
