728
00:50:21,285 --> 00:50:21,686
Speaker 1: Whatever.

729
00:50:21,866 --> 00:50:23,390
Speaker 1: But it was the one that mattered.

730
00:50:23,510 --> 00:50:34,150
Speaker 0: So even if Boss was technically the first worker placement game we know of, Kalis is the first one that mattered because Kalis is when the whole board gaming world started talking about worker placement as a concept.

731
00:50:35,870 --> 00:50:37,092
Speaker 0: But we could then.

732
00:50:37,292 --> 00:50:51,230
Speaker 0: so you can break complex games down into a collection of simpler games, but then you can break each one of those games down into a collection of mechanics and they can break those mechanics down to Scott's very short list.

733
00:50:53,211 --> 00:51:04,850
Speaker 0: And I have been idly starting to factorize Cobalt Core and you can go pretty deep and do a very long exercise if you do this.

734
00:51:06,571 --> 00:51:13,010
Speaker 1: It's like any game you think of is like, you know, and sometimes games really are like literally the same.

735
00:51:13,210 --> 00:51:19,042
Speaker 1: It's like you look at something like, you know, Civ 5 and Civ 6 and it's like, sure, it's.

736
00:51:19,363 --> 00:51:23,030
Speaker 1: it's like there are differences between them, right?

737
00:51:23,050 --> 00:51:24,073
Speaker 1: They're not the same game.

738
00:51:24,254 --> 00:51:24,895
Speaker 1: You don't right.

739
00:51:24,915 --> 00:51:25,256
Speaker 1: They're not.

740
00:51:25,718 --> 00:51:27,824
Speaker 1: it's not like, oh, yes, I would always play one.

741
00:51:27,864 --> 00:51:29,850
Speaker 1: The other one, there's no reason for it to exist anymore.

742
00:51:30,051 --> 00:51:31,535
Speaker 1: The rules are significantly different.

743
00:51:31,555 --> 00:51:32,718
Speaker 1: They are very different games.

744
00:51:33,180 --> 00:51:36,810
Speaker 1: But if you factorize them, it's like the same ingredients.

745
00:51:36,930 --> 00:51:37,151
Speaker 1: Yep.

746
00:51:37,411 --> 00:51:37,592
Speaker 1: Right.

747
00:51:37,872 --> 00:51:38,153
Speaker 1: It's like.

748
00:51:38,394 --> 00:51:45,670
Speaker 1: it's like you could take the same ingredients and make two recipes, but in different proportions of the ingredients and different preparations of them.

749
00:51:46,251 --> 00:51:47,414
Speaker 1: But the ingredients are the same.

750
00:51:48,316 --> 00:51:53,710
Speaker 1: And so basically what it's like what we're saying in the panel is that it's not that every game is the same.

751
00:51:53,790 --> 00:51:54,832
Speaker 1: It's the opposite of that.

752
00:51:54,952 --> 00:52:05,010
Speaker 1: It's that there is not that many ingredients in making games and all games are basically made from the same very short list of ingredients in different combinations and amounts and flavors.

753
00:52:05,110 --> 00:52:05,230
Speaker 0: Yep.

754
00:52:05,691 --> 00:52:29,970
Speaker 0: And I think it also means I have found trying to factorize Cobalt Core, there is a lot of value in thinking about games this way, even though I've definitely encountered a large number of game designers and developers over the years who get really mad when you describe their game as other game crossed with other other game, like they get really mad about that.

755
00:52:30,690 --> 00:52:38,230
Speaker 1: But I think, you know, if you're if you're trying to sell a game, you know, if you're making a work of art, right, you don't want to, you know, even outside of games.

756
00:52:38,511 --> 00:52:40,096
Speaker 1: But, you know, you don't want to be.

757
00:52:40,116 --> 00:52:44,270
Speaker 1: if you're making music, you don't want someone to say, oh, you're just Nirvana plus a little bit of that.

758
00:52:44,710 --> 00:52:44,871
Speaker 1: But at

759
00:52:44,891 --> 00:53:03,290
Speaker 0: the same time, people who study games and also people like us, if someone describes a game as these three games crossed together, that is usually way more useful, interesting and valuable to me than them trying to describe the game as its own like equation or its own like acting like it's a prime number.

760
00:53:03,911 --> 00:53:04,091
Speaker 1: Right.

761
00:53:04,131 --> 00:53:11,064
Speaker 1: But people don't want to be seen as, you know, unoriginal, uncreative, just copying or they also don't want.

762
00:53:11,425 --> 00:53:14,390
Speaker 1: And also the other thing people don't want is from a marketing perspective.

763
00:53:14,771 --> 00:53:14,951
Speaker 1: Right.

764
00:53:15,011 --> 00:53:24,270
Speaker 1: It's like if you say that my music is like, you know, Nirvana plus Queen, then people who don't like Nirvana or don't like Queen are going to not even look at my game.

