00:53:24,410 --> 00:53:33,790
Speaker 1: Even though if you tell me your game and people who do like Nirvana and or Queen might come in with twisted expectations now and also bounce off.

766
00:53:33,910 --> 00:53:34,191
Speaker 1: True.

767
00:53:34,431 --> 00:53:44,290
Speaker 0: But simultaneously, if you tell me this game is World of Warcraft crossed with anything, as long as that statement is accurate, now I don't have to bounce off.

768
00:53:44,430 --> 00:53:45,339
Speaker 0: I know to avoid it.

769
00:53:46,681 --> 00:53:47,021
Speaker 1: Right.

770
00:53:47,382 --> 00:53:51,748
Speaker 1: It's it's good information if it's accurate for, you know, enjoyers of artwork.

771
00:53:51,808 --> 00:53:59,700
Speaker 1: But I'm saying creators of artwork from their perspective have very good reason to not like that kind of conversation out in the world.

772
00:54:00,561 --> 00:54:00,781
Speaker 0: Yep.

773
00:54:01,162 --> 00:54:10,580
Speaker 0: But Tycho and Penny Arcade recently wrote something, not going to pull the whole thing up, but he was talking about PAL World and the somewhat...

774
00:54:10,740 --> 00:54:14,080
Speaker 1: PAL World, which is literally just the salad of every game ever in one space.

775
00:54:14,100 --> 00:54:20,540
Speaker 0: But he was responding to the kind of extreme vitriol that's been growing from a subset of gamers about that game.

776
00:54:22,261 --> 00:54:43,340
Speaker 0: And to summarize what he said, that sort of like remix mashing together of existing popular things into something else is a very important function of gaming in that it is synthesis like that that leads to patterns and leads to memory and like the long term development of games.

777
00:54:44,701 --> 00:54:53,680
Speaker 1: Well, the point we're making with the panel is that whether you are trying to or not, games are always synthesis because there aren't that many ingredients.

